  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nFriday night, convection is forecast to continue developing south \ninto the CWA, with the front entering the northern zones sometime \nSaturday morning.  For those who prefer cooler weather, this is a \nrare treat, considering that 7/30-8/2 is climatologically the \nwarmest time of the year for KMAF.  The big question is how much \ncooling will accompany this mid-summer cold front?  Some models, \nsuch as the NAM, depict highs Saturday in the 80s, whereas the GFS \nis around 10 F warmer.  The NBM, slower to respond as would be \nexpected with a blend, only shaves around 5 F off of Friday's highs. \nAssuming that diurnal heating will slow/modify the front somewhat as \nit tries to move in Saturday, warmer solutions seem more realistic. \nW/the strength of the cold front increasing with time as the event \nnears, so have rain chances, rendering Saturday afternoon the best \nchances of rain this forecast.  Chances move south with the front \nSaturday night.\n\nSunday, under deep northerly flow aloft, and northeasterly flow at \nthe surface, the NBM keeps highs similar to Saturday's, with \nlingering convective chances mainly south of I-10.  The rest of the \nweek, the ridge will oscillate around the Four Corners, resulting in \na warmup not so much due to the upper ridge as the absence of the \nfront.  By Wednesday, highs should be back up to ~ 3-5 F above \nnormal.",
